<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no">
  <title>Designing a Minimal Research Log Template</title>
  <link href="../../css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <link href="../css/blog.css" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body>
  <main class="post-wrapper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <article class="col-lg-10">
          <a class="post-back" href="../../index.html">← Back</a>

          <header class="post-header">
            <h1>Designing a Minimal Research Log Template</h1>
            <div class="post-meta-block">
              <div><strong>Author:</strong> Kuan-Hsun Tu</div>
              <div><strong>Published:</strong> 04/27/2024</div>
              <div><strong>Word count:</strong> 820</div>
              <div><strong>Reading time:</strong> 4 min</div>
            </div>
          </header>

          <div class="post-content">
            <p>
              A minimal writing system should feel friendly to jump into, even on busy days.
              This template keeps the <strong>structure lightweight</strong> while highlighting the essentials:
              <em>intent</em>, <em>signal</em>, and clear follow-up actions. Most importantly, it matches the typography
              and colors used across the rest of the site so the transition into long-form notes feels natural.
            </p>

            <div class="note-block">
              <p>
                <strong>Note:</strong> The metadata block above is intentionally dense. It is optimized for quick scanning,
                and you can update it manually after drafting. Feel free to extend it with tags or experiment identifiers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <p>
              The template leans on a handful of principles:
              focus on a single takeaway, capture context before diving into results,
              and link out to raw artifacts (dashboards, notebooks, or simulations) so the narrative remains concise.
              When I mention tools—like <a href="https://wandb.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Weights &amp; Biases</a>—I link directly to the source
              for future readers who may not know the reference.
            </p>

            <h2>Session Goals</h2>
            <p>
              Start each entry with a quick outline of what success looks like. For example,
              validating a new reward schedule or reworking a perception stack. Having this section at the top
              keeps the rest of the document honest; if it drifts, I know to split the entry into a new post.
            </p>

            <ol>
              <li>Summarize the current system state and open issues.</li>
              <li>Document the planned experiments and expected outcomes.</li>
              <li>Log the surprises—positive or negative—and flag follow-ups.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Implementation Notes</h2>
            <p>
              Inline code snippets such as <code>docker compose up --build</code> sit comfortably in paragraphs.
              Longer command sequences or configuration fragments live inside code blocks for clarity:
            </p>

            <pre><code class="language-bash"># Spin up training workers with the latest policy image
python tools/launch_workers.py \
  --cluster prod-euw1 \
  --workers 12 \
  --policy builds/policy-2024-04-27.pt</code></pre>

            <p>
              The same idea applies to configuration diffs, pseudo-code, or script highlights. Keep things focused on the change,
              and pair each snippet with a short description so readers understand why it matters.
            </p>

            <h2>Key Observations</h2>
            <p>
              Lists work well for capturing structured takeaways. Bullets can nest when needed,
              but keeping them brief encourages quicker iteration on future passes:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Latency dropped by 18% after pinning inference to isolated CPU cores.</li>
              <li>
                Policy generalization improved once we diversified the reset curriculum:
                <ul>
                  <li>Added three random object placements per episode.</li>
                  <li>Injected depth sensor noise drawn from lab captures.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>We still need better logging around failure recovery triggers.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Visual cues make scanning easier. For media, the template ensures images scale gracefully across devices:
            </p>

            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/1200x600?text=Experiment+Dashboard" alt="Mock dashboard preview illustrating experiment metrics">

            <p>
              Wrap up with decisions, risks, and next steps. Readers should leave knowing how to reproduce the results,
              what to watch out for, and which questions remain unanswered.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <footer class="blog-footer">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-10">
          <p class="footer-text">© <span id="current-year"></span> Kuan-Hsun Tu · Thanks for reading.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    document.getElementById('current-year').textContent = new Date().getFullYear();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
